### Summary of Key Insights
The retrieved evidence demonstrates that mosquito bed nets—both treated and untreated—are highly effective public health tools that do not pose significant health risks to humans. A study in The Gambia found that untreated nets in good condition provide 51% protection against malaria, while a Cochrane Review established that insecticide-treated nets (ITNs) significantly reduce malaria transmission and child mortality. Furthermore, the World Health Organization (WHO) and the CDC state that the pyrethroid insecticides used on ITNs are rigorously tested, safe for human contact, and pose very low health risks, with only occasional, mild, and transient side effects reported. The minimal risks associated with proper net usage are vastly outweighed by the life-saving protection they offer against the severe dangers of malaria.

### Conclusion
Based on the overwhelming scientific consensus and clinical evidence, the claim that sleeping under a mosquito bed net is ineffective and harmful to human health is completely false. Both treated and untreated nets are proven to be highly effective at preventing malaria, and the chemical treatments used are thoroughly evaluated and deemed safe for daily human use. Therefore, the final decision is `refuted`.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
Scientific consensus strongly refutes the assertion that mosquito bed nets are ineffective and harmful. A [PubMed study](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11706649/) demonstrates that even untreated nets in good condition provide up to 51% protection against malaria, while a [Cochrane Review](https://www.cochrane.org/CD000363/INFECTN_insecticide-treated-nets-preventing-malaria) confirms that insecticide-treated nets (ITNs) are highly effective, significantly reducing malaria transmission and child mortality. Regarding safety, the [World Health Organization](https://www.afro.who.int/news/treated-mosquito-nets-are-safe-and-effective) and the [CDC](https://www.cdc.gov/malaria/malaria_worldwide/reduction/itn.html) state that the pyrethroid insecticides used on ITNs undergo rigorous safety testing and pose no significant health risks to humans, with only occasional, mild, and transient side effects reported. Ultimately, the life-saving benefits of using these nets vastly outweigh any minimal risks, which are further mitigated by proper usage as detailed in [toxicological research](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10759305/).